rsonally I  really like the hexagon shaped ones with  the curved roofs for me that feels like  the right balance and then between these  there will be in real life tiled roofs  that curve to the sides of these and  also curved inwards themselves a little  bit to create these really cool core  curved structures and this was really  difficult to recreate in game because we  also don't have any separate tile roof  pieces so the piece that I ended up  using for this tiled roof is actually  the full-stop from the alphabets setting  or the alphabet sets I suppose of  scenery pieces which in hindsight I have  to spoil was a terrible idea  the  while I was building this whole  structure I didn't really test this but  as soon as I finished the structure and  I zoomed out a bit I realized that the  render distance of these pieces is  abysmal and as soon as you zoomed out a  bit the whole structure would completely  disappear which I'll get to fixing in a  minute but yeah that made using these  pieces kind of impractical so as good as  I think it looks because it does look  surprisingly convincing I think for a  tile roof with these sort of concrete  dividing decoration elements I think it  looks quite decent but once you zoom out  the whole thing disappears so eventually  I ended up having to sink some larger  pieces in there which disappear less  quickly just to make sort of an LOD for  this whole pavilion which is something  that I'll do in a minute once I'm done  with these pillars and the fence around  people go in as well now I always get a  lot of comments from people why I'm not  just using a 3d modelling program to do  some stuff that I do and for this one I  was definitely feeling like that's  because realistically what I ended up  doing is making a lower detail version  of this pavilion right underneath the  existing one just to make sure that it  doesn't completely disappear when you  zoom out which is something that if  you're working on game design and you're  making 3d models you have to provide  these Elodie's to them so yeah maybe I  did go into a 3d model development  direction with this one aside from that  though I do feel like this was  surprisingly easy a lot of it just comes  down to the rotational symmetry and  making a good cutouts of the shape of  the building that you want before you  build anything else because the way that  it works is just you make one side and  then you copy it over to the other side  and start rotating it to create the  whole structure so when they say in this  case since I was building a hexagon  shaped pavilion I had to make sure to  build 1/6 of the pavilion just finish  that and copy it over to the other side  and then rotate it  fill in the other five parts of the  pavilion unfortunately it was a little  bit off grid so fitting these pieces  together without leaving any gaps  between them was a bit difficult but in  the end I think it connects quite  seamlessly and whatever the case I'm  really happy with how this turned out it  definitely turned out a bit better than  I was expecting and I say that with full  experience of trying to make Asian roofs  in a planet game since I tried to do the  same for fantasy belly in planet coaster  which I also think turned out a little  bit less nice than this building and I  didn't even dare at the time to try and  build a Chinese pavilion like this with  six sides so definitely happy that I  tried this and then it worked out as  well as it did if not just for the one  little issue that it disappears a bit  too quickly when you zoom out but I  think the lower detail elements that I  added underneath these roof tiles do  make up for that and once you zoom out  you just basically see a lower detail  version of this pavilion  now around this one side of it will be  the habitats and the other side which is  here will be a garden and there aren't  gonna be any exhibits or buildings in  this space I'm really just trying to  provide this pavilion with a Chinese  style garden context just because I  think that's what realistically these  kinds of pavilions should fit into which  is also I suppose inspired a bit by pi d  dizer I have to admit as much as I've  made videos on Japanese gardens I don't  really have the experience of Chinese  gardens that I do for the Japanese ones  I haven't traveled to China I haven't  been to as many real Chinese gardens as  I have for Japanese garden so really  can't speak with as much authority here  but in the end I do want to create a  garden setting for this pavilion because  you'll very commonly find these kinds of  pavilions in traditional Chinese gardens  and in fact I think there are lots of  similarities between Chinese and  Japanese gardens if you look overall  they are both meant to be a sort of  miniature replica of a natural landscape  using a lot of rock work and bushes and  trees and also very easy exactly winding  paths and water elements to try and  create a sort of smaller natural  landscape which i think is really cool  and what I like about Japanese gardens  in the first place and how I think in  plants ooh specifically a lot of the  foliage and work work and pathways that  work for Japanese gardens could just as  easily work for Chinese gardens as well  really a major difference that I tried  to incorporate a bit here as well is  that while Japanese gardens as even  though there are many different types  are really overall meant to be walked  through and they don't really feature as  much architecture whereas Chinese  gardens typically tend to feature many  more buildings and are much more seen  from inside a building setting rather  than strolled through as you do with  many Japanese strolling garden to tea  gardens or lake based gardens so that's  I guess have made a difference  but for both of these I think there's a  fair set of scenery out there in the  game to make these things not to mention  that the cherry blossom tree is  absolutely beautiful and I tried to use  this conservatively so there's only one  cherry blossom tree which is in that  very strategic position at this junction  of all of these paths at the end of the  bridge which I think works out quite  well but yeah there's a really beautiful  tree probably my favorite one in the  game obviously they're really beautiful  in real life but this transition in the  game is just so beautifully pink I  really can't put it in a different way  it's just really nice to look at and I  think it even goes very well with the  path textures of this kind of assorted  cobblestone path and the same goes for a  lot of the other foliage I'm not as big  a fan of the Japanese maple they've  definitely gone for more of an orange  color whereas I do like the dark red  purple is almost : that they often have  in real life but yeah I ended up putting  some Japanese maples in there as well  might not be the most Chinese thing but  at the same time it just works for the  overall look of the garden not to  mention that at the end of the day you  can't really make a completely authentic  Japanese of Chinese gardens these things  are very very much based on ceremonial  and cultural traditions which you can  never really recreate yourself  especially me just being a dumb  Westerner so it's really all just  inspired by it and trying to take the  aesthetic and mold it into a zoo here  although at the same time I'm really  trying to keep the garden esque elements  to just the path areas and keep the  actual habitats of the bears as natural  as possible speaking of which I actually  skipped through a whole section of me  working on the habitat itself just  because I didn't record it it was a lot  of me looking around the habitats trying  to do some stuff trying to figure out  how I wanted to build it it didn't work  as well for a time-lapse but yeah in the  meantime I did work on the habitat  itself as well now here kind of running  behind I started working on some roof  sets and this is really giving me  sometimes Isabella flashbacks because I  tried making pretty  much the exact same roof sent in fantasy  valley a combination of three main roof  pieces to make these Asian typical  curved roofs with a straight piece with  a slight curve to it and a larger steep  piece to try and fill in larger roof  structures and finally the most  difficult thing to get right the corner  piece which curves upward and which is  quite difficult to make it work but for  this one at least I learned from the  mistakes that I made on the pavilion so  I made it out of different pieces that I  tested beforehand and really wouldn't  disappear too quickly and the piece that  I ended up going with to make the roof  tiles out of is the letter e so that's  kind of a meme honestly but yeah it just  worked out that the eave was the best  shape to make these roof tiles out of so  I ended up going with the E and then for  the corners the these stone sections I'm  just taking some limestone pillars now  the thing about these types of Asian  roofs is that I can really only try to  imitate the look that these roofs have  from a distance because obviously I  don't have actual roof tiles but Chinese  and Japanese roof tiles are very similar  they're very different from most western  roof tiles in that they are you've got  round cap pieces and then round valley  pieces and they both intermingled it's  it's like a mix of the two of them so  often if you look at an Asian roof you  can find at the bottom of the roof round  pieces and then a half moon freshen  shape downwards and then a round piece  again etc so these roofs tend to have  very typical look even if you look at  them from a distance and that's  basically the look that I tried to  imitate by sinking these EES inward a  little bit and then raising the other  one up again kind of intermittently is  that a word I don't know anyway you get  the drift I'm trying to make these roofs  look vaguely Asian even though they are  made out of the letter e and also that  did include putting some full stops at  the end of these roofs I know I said I  wouldn't use  . anymore but for this one just to have  that detail when you look up close i  decided to put these at the end just to  at least imitate a little bit the  decorations that you have at the end of  these tiled roofs because they're very  very decorated i find in general even  that while a lot of european  architecture really focuses on the walls  themselves and kind of leave the roofs  with maybe just a few decorations some  railings and some dormers Asian and  Chinese architecture focuses much more  on the roof the roof is a much larger  parts of the general structure of the  building and is much more detailed  whereas the lower sections of the  building are much more simple at least  as far as the attending goes so here too  I'll be spending most of my time on this  building just trying to perfect the roof  and then the rest is basically just  filling to me whoa I just destroyed my  whole setup anyway the rest is just  basically filling in everything else I'm  trying to reset up my pop filter and  everything here in the meantime I'm  gonna try to actually pay attention to  the video shouldn't be too bad but yeah  that that is basically my general  approach to this building now something  that I didn't really talk about but  which i think is relevant to point out  as well  is that this building was mostly  inspired by a building in Japan actually  in Shinjuku Gyoen which is a park in  Shinjuku in Tokyo there is a Taiwanese  pavilion in the middle of the park which  is a really cool building to view the  garden from and it was actually gifted  by Japanese people in Taiwan I believe  but very much Taiwanese style more so  than a Japanese style and I liked the  idea of having a building to view the  gone and from it's much more of a a  Chinese sort of thing but I think it  works out really well for this habitat  as well this will be the main viewing  platform to see the habitats from it is  right next to the shelter  which I did shelter a bit more with some  extra work work so the animals don't  completely have to be in view but yeah  it's I think right in front of this is  where the animals will be spending most  of their time where the food will be  delivered and things like that  so I think this would be a good viewing  place to see the habitat from a good  place to see animals and a good framing  for the habitat as a home because the  the perspective that you have from here  of the bridge and of all of the foliage  around the place I think looks really  cool so that's why I ended up putting  this building here and I just like the  picture of looking at the garden in the  distance with the pillars and  decorations of the building that you're  standing in in the foreground I think it  provides this really cool foreground  background interaction that I really  like so that's something that I ended up  going further with this building and  yeah that's my general idea behind the  placements and design of this building I  was also inspired by in general  Taiwanese temples but I can't really pin  this down on anything specific I have to  say that really all I did was open up a  Google Images tab and a search Taiwanese  temple and keep a bunch of images as  reference for ideas to see you know what  the color scheme should be like how I  should do it things like that I often  get the question of what inspires me  where I get my ideas from and honestly a  lot of it is religious googling at  random and seeing what you like seeing  what you can draw ideas from and what  you can base general structures on  because obviously I'm not an expert in  Chinese architectures and having  references images that that's too many  plural words whether it really should  have been singular anyway having these  references images why do I keep doing  this having these reference images in  the background can really help to try  and get an 